member.summary:
Execute filter algorythm: applies the HAVING condition (the single free boolean
) on the projected result table, keeping only the grouped rows for which it
evaluates to true. When no HAVING is set the table is returned unchanged.

RDFGroupByCondition is a single SPARQL GROUP BY condition, unifying the three legal forms under one shape so the modifier no longer has to spread a grouping condition across several parallel structures:
a bare grouping variable 'GROUP BY ?v' — is null;
a named grouping expression 'GROUP BY (expr AS ?v)' — is true, '?v' is a real, projectable variable;
an anonymous grouping expression 'GROUP BY (expr)' — is false, backed by a reserved (synthetic) variable that must NOT surface in the results.

summary.para:
            Step kinds (see ):


Link — a single predicate IRI (the atomic step);

Sequence — an ordered composition of children (P1/P2/…);

Alternative — a union of children (P1|P2|…);

NegatedPropertySet — one hop over any predicate NOT in the given set (!(a|^b)).

            A group is implicit in the tree structure: (a/b)+ is a Sequence carrying the OneOrMore cardinality,
            ^(a|b) is an Alternative carrying the inverse flag. When a decoration would conflict with the
            evaluation order (cardinality is applied before inverse), the combinator wraps the node in a single-child
            Sequence acting as the explicit group.

member.summary:
The variable carrying the endpoint IRI when the SERVICE specifier is a variable (SERVICE ?ep {…});
null when the endpoint is a concrete IRI. When set, the engine resolves it against the surrounding
solution bindings and queries each distinct IRI in turn by overwriting 's address.

summary.para:
            RDFPathEngine evaluates SPARQL 1.1 property paths over a datasource. A property path is a recursive
            step tree () and every node denotes a BINARY RELATION over
            the datasource terms (the set of (start, end) pairs the sub-path connects). The engine computes that
            relation compositionally:


Link — the one-hop adjacency of a predicate (with the memoized transitive closure for + / *);

Sequence — relational composition of its children (lazy frontier expansion);

Alternative — union of its children;

NegatedPropertySet — one hop over any predicate not in the set;

cardinality (? * +) and inverse (^) decorate any node.

            A path NODE is an : a resource everywhere a hop can continue, but possibly a
            literal when it is the terminal object of the last hop (or the source of an inverse hop). Only resources
            carry outgoing edges, so literals naturally behave as sinks.

member.summary:
An explicit, finite binary relation over datasource terms: the (node hash → successors) adjacency map
plus the registry mapping each hash back to its term. Produced by materializing a composite sub-path;
consumed by the closure builder and by inverse reversal.

Parses a single DataBlockValue — an IRI, an RDF literal, a numeric/boolean literal, or the
UNDEF placeholder. UNDEF yields null (the model's representation of an unbound binding);
every other token is read through the shared term-reader (so prologue BASE/PREFIX resolution applies).

summary.para:
SPARQL grammar: GroupGraphPattern ::= '{' ( SubSelect | GroupGraphPatternSub ) '}'.
Both alternatives are handled: a leading SELECT opens a SubSelect (a nested SELECT query, parsed by
), otherwise the body is a GroupGraphPatternSub.

Parses the body of an ORDER BY clause (the ORDER keyword has already been consumed by
) and attaches the resulting ordering modifiers via
.

RDFSPARQLTermResolver supplies base-IRI and prefix-to-namespace resolution to the Turtle term-level parsers (ParseURI, ParseQNameOrBoolean, ...) while the SPARQL parser is running. It is the SPARQL counterpart of RDFGraphTermResolver: where the graph-backed resolver reads base/prefixes LIVE out of an RDFGraph, this one is fully AUTONOMOUS and accumulates the state declared by the query's own prologue:
the base IRI set by a BASE <...> directive;
the prefix-to-namespace bindings introduced by every PREFIX label: <...> directive.
